"""Course manuscript prose. Eight planned main pages; supplementary methods separate."""
from reportlab.lib.units import mm
from production.course_evidence import *
from production.report_equations import equation

def eq(a,name): equation(a,ASSETS,name)
def table(a,rows,caption,widths=None):
    if widths: widths=[166*mm*w/sum(widths) for w in widths]
    a.table(rows,caption,widths)

def write_main(a):
    a.h('Abstract')
    a.p('Human interception requires coordinating movement with a moving target, yet repeated attempts vary within and between people. We investigate whether a compact representation of a participant can support a model of this variability. Using 4,732 free-eye-movement trials from 28 participants, we compared variational autoencoders (VAE), conditional VAE (CVAE), conditional autoencoders (CAE), and spline coefficients compressed by principal component analysis (PCA), with three or eight latent coordinates. Four participant-held-out rotations evaluated trajectory reconstruction and generation from separate context trials. Spline + PCA achieved the lowest reconstruction error. For generation at eight dimensions, VAE and CVAE improved on spline + PCA in both kinematic-feature and full-trajectory distribution comparisons. Their full-trajectory energy discrepancies were 0.0548 and 0.0549, compared with 0.0981 for spline + PCA; their difference was not significant. Replacing a participant\'s own latent center with population or other-participant centers worsened feature fidelity. However, directly resampling stored context trajectories achieved a lower mean full-trajectory discrepancy than the compact models. We provide an interactive dashboard, with VAE at eight dimensions as the default, and distinguish useful session-specific compression from a complete account of individual movement variability.')
    a.h('1 Introduction')
    a.p('Intercepting a moving object requires predicting where and when the object will be reached while adjusting an ongoing movement. Repeated movements can differ in their onset, duration, curvature and speed profile, even within one participant. Modeling only an average trajectory omits this variability. Our practical objective is therefore to represent distributions of interception movements, including both their detailed paths and interpretable kinematic features, rather than to predict a single deterministic response.')
    a.p('A compact participant representation would make such a model easier to inspect and manipulate. We ask whether averaging a participant\'s encoded context trials into a small vector, termed a fingerprint, provides useful information for generating the remainder of that participant\'s session. Three coordinates address the original compact-representation objective, while eight provide a comparison with greater capacity. This definition makes the fingerprint an operational summary of recordings; it does not assume that its individual coordinates correspond to named movement strategies.')
    a.p('Variational autoencoders learn nonlinear latent representations with a probabilistic training objective [1]. Conditional variants additionally receive observed task variables [2]. We compare these approaches with a deterministic neural autoencoder and a statistical pipeline that fits smooth spline coefficients before applying PCA. This comparison separates the value of a nonlinear decoder, probabilistic regularization and explicit task conditioning. Reconstruction first tests how much of an observed trajectory survives compression. Generation then tests whether a participant\'s context representation produces appropriate variability on separate trials. A subsequent full-trajectory analysis complements feature-based distances by preserving the ordered path.')
    a.p('The project deliverable is a dashboard that exposes the evaluated models, generated movements and benchmark results. Model selection follows the intended generative use: a model can reproduce an observed path accurately yet generate an inadequate distribution. We therefore report both successes and the stronger empirical reference obtained by retaining and resampling context trajectories. Additional pipeline details, corrected comparisons and secondary investigations appear in the accompanying appendix.')
    a.page()
    a.h('2 Data and preprocessing')
    a.p('Jason Friedman\'s laboratory supplied finger-tracking recordings from an interception task. Participants moved from a starting region to a fixed interception region while a circular target approached horizontally (Figure 1). The target started at one of three categories on either side. Each category had its own speed range, so starting position and speed were related by the experimental design. We retained the free-eye-movement condition (condition 2). The five model condition values were a three-element one-hot starting-category vector, a side indicator and executed target speed normalized by screen width. Participant identity and trial outcomes were not condition inputs.')
    a.figure(OUT/'presentation_assets/task_schematic.png','<b>Figure 1.</b> Task illustration adapted in the project presentation from the supplied display: lower starting region, upper interception region and horizontally approaching target. The display illustrates the task, not the tracking apparatus.',53*mm)
    a.p('Of 4,763 available trials from 28 participants, four lacked an arrival event and 27 arrived more than one second after the target window ended. The resulting 4,732 trials included early arrivals and fixation warnings. Twenty-six participants contributed 171-180 trials; the remaining two contributed 60 and 28 from 60 and 29 available recordings, respectively. The median retained count was 179.5. Each of four fixed rotations used 17 training, four validation and seven test participants, and tested each participant once. All learned transformations used training data; validation data selected settings and checkpoints.')
    a.p('Finger positions were recorded in three dimensions at 240 Hz. Duplicate frame counters were averaged and missing counters linearly interpolated onto a regular grid. A fourth-order 10 Hz Butterworth filter applied forward and backward smoothed positions. The paired target recordings identified target-motion onset. We modeled the interval from that event to the last tracker sample, retaining waiting and movement within this window. The last sample was an arrival proxy, on average 26.2 ms before the MATLAB arrival event. The earlier target-appearance interval was outside this modeled window.')
    a.p('Trials had different durations and sample counts. For a filtered window of L samples, the original phases were i/(L - 1). A cubic interpolant S evaluated at j/99 produced 100 points, translated to start at zero:')
    eq(a,'resample')
    a.p('The planar x-y coordinates formed a 100 x 2 array, flattened in time order to 200 neural inputs. This resampling preserves relative temporal progression; it does not make durations equal in seconds or scale spatial amplitude. Initiation and movement durations were separate supervised targets, transformed as log(t + 0.001) and standardized on training trials. Onset used a sustained three-frame crossing of 5 tracker units/s in 3-D speed. There were 357 zero-initiation labels (7.5%), including movements already above threshold at target motion. These movements remained in the analysis. Tracker units and event assumptions awaiting laboratory confirmation are listed in Appendix A1.')
    a.page()
    a.h('3 Compact trajectory models')
    a.h('3.1 Spline coefficients, PCA and timing regression',True)
    a.p('The statistical model first expressed each resampled coordinate as a cubic B-spline. Five interior knots at 1/6, ..., 5/6 produced nine basis functions per axis and a common design matrix B of size 100 x 9. Separate least-squares fits gave nine x coefficients and nine y coefficients:')
    eq(a,'spline')
    a.p('Concatenating these coefficients yielded one 18-value row per trial. Stacking only training rows formed the N<sub>train</sub> x 18 matrix on which PCA was fitted. Thus PCA compressed spline coefficients, rather than the 100 sampled points or individual basis functions. Validation reconstruction MSE selected between raw and standardized coefficient variants. Let m and d denote the effective centering and scaling vectors (d = 1 for raw coefficients), and let V contain the n retained PCA directions:')
    eq(a,'pca')
    a.p('Decoding split the reconstructed coefficient vector into two nine-value vectors and multiplied each by B, restoring 100 x 2 positions. A separate Ridge regression predicted two standardized log-duration targets from the n scores and five task conditions. Training-only input scaling and validation-selected regularization completed this timing head. Conditions therefore affected spline timing, but not its spatial decoder. Appendix A2 gives the matrix dimensions and inverse transformations.')
    a.h('3.2 Variational and deterministic neural models',True)
    a.p('Neural models standardized each of the 200 coordinate inputs using training-trial statistics. Encoder and decoder each used two 256-unit ReLU hidden layers. A variational encoder predicted an n-dimensional mean and log variance, with training samples obtained by the reparameterization z = mu + sigma multiplied elementwise by standard-normal noise [1]. The decoder predicted 200 standardized coordinates and two standardized log durations. Its per-trial objective was:')
    eq(a,'loss')
    a.p('Here v denotes the standardized trajectory, y the two standardized timing targets and c the task conditions. Both squared-error terms are <i>sums</i> over coordinates, followed by a batch mean; replacing them with separately averaged MSEs would change their relative weight. The KL term regularizes the posterior toward a standard normal, with beta increased from zero to one over 50 epochs. The conditional VAE supplied the five conditions to both encoder and decoder. Our VAE used the same framework with these inputs masked to zero. The CAE used a deterministic code and omitted the KL term.')
    a.p('Training used Adam (learning rate 0.001), batches of 64, inverse-participant-trial-count sampling, gradient clipping and validation-based learning-rate reduction and early stopping. Three seeds (42, 43, 44) were evaluated per neural model and rotation. The matched comparisons in this report use n = 3 and n = 8. Reconstruction decodes the observed trial\'s latent mean or deterministic code; it receives the trial trajectory but not its physical timing targets. The condition-only Ridge comparator receives task conditions without an observed trajectory or personal code. Appendix A3 provides the exact training and inference stages.')
    a.page()
    a.h('4 Generation and statistical evaluation')
    a.p('To evaluate a previously unseen participant, we split that person\'s trials approximately in half into context and query sets, stratified by starting category and side. The split was shared across models. Only context trajectories formed the participant fingerprint: their encoded means were averaged to an n-value center. We sampled 120 latent vectors around that center using a covariance estimated from training participants:')
    eq(a,'fingerprint')
    a.p('The shared covariance combined within-participant variation of training codes with mean posterior variance for variational models and a small diagonal regularizer. CAE and spline omitted posterior variance. This is a participant-specific center with a shared noise model, not a separately estimated covariance for every participant. Generation used the query task-condition mixture where applicable, but never query trajectories or timing labels. The VAE ignored those conditions. Appendix A4 specifies the covariance and sampling procedure.')
    a.h('4.1 Reconstruction and feature distributions',True)
    a.p('Reconstruction error was the mean squared coordinate difference across 100 x 2 positions, in squared tracker units. For generation, eleven features described timing, speed, path geometry, endpoints and a heuristic speed-peak count (Appendix A5). Recorded features used movement-onset-to-end paths. Generated features used predicted initiation and movement durations to crop the generated path and recover a time scale. Thus feature fidelity also depended on timing predictions.')
    a.p('For each participant and feature, the Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) statistic measured the largest difference between recorded and generated empirical cumulative distributions. We averaged the eleven KS statistics. Joint feature comparisons used energy discrepancy [3] and squared maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) with a Gaussian kernel [4], after training-derived feature standardization. KS values here are discrepancies, not hypothesis-test p-values.')
    a.h('4.2 Complete trajectory distributions',True)
    a.p('The follow-up analysis compared entire generated and query paths without additional cropping or filtering. Flattening a 100 x 2 path and dividing by the square root of 200 made Euclidean distance equal to root mean squared coordinate difference. Energy discrepancy compared cross-distribution distances with within-distribution variation:')
    eq(a,'energy')
    a.p('Within-sample means included diagonal zeros for energy. MMD used a Gaussian kernel, with bandwidth fixed from training paths and within-sample diagonals excluded. An axis-balanced sensitivity analysis divided each coordinate axis by its training RMS variation. Lower discrepancies indicate closer distributions. They are not accuracy percentages. Repeating the context mean and resampling stored context paths provided additional diagnostic references.')
    a.h('4.3 Aggregation and comparisons',True)
    a.p('We averaged neural seeds within each participant and weighted all 28 participants equally. Paired two-sided Pratt Wilcoxon tests compared participant scores, with differences rounded to 12 decimals. Benjamini-Hochberg (BH) correction [5] covered the original 140 model comparisons (10 pairs, two dimensions, seven endpoints). The new trajectory analysis used a separate family of 80 comparisons (10 pairs, two dimensions, two metrics, two geometries). We also report Holm sensitivity. These are exploratory comparisons on overlapping training rotations, rather than independent replications. Neither the capacity comparisons nor the context-resampling reference received new significance tests. Appendix A6 gives estimators, assumptions and corrected values.')
    a.page()
    a.h('5 Reconstruction and generated kinematic features')
    a.p('Reconstruction established the fidelity of each compressed representation before testing generation. Spline + PCA had the lowest cohort-average trajectory MSE at both capacities (Table 1). Increasing capacity reduced all four models\' mean errors descriptively. The best neural reconstruction differed by capacity: VAE at three dimensions and CAE at eight. Therefore a single reconstruction ranking does not determine the generative model selected for the dashboard.')
    table(a,[['Model','n = 3 MSE','n = 8 MSE']]+[[LABELS[f],f'{value(f,3,"trajectory_mse"):.6f}',f'{value(f,8,"trajectory_mse"):.6f}'] for f in FAMILIES],'<b>Table 1.</b> Participant-averaged reconstruction MSE; lower is better. Spline + PCA beat each neural model at each dimension under BH correction over 140 tests (all q &lt; 0.0031). The spline-versus-CAE n = 8 comparison did not survive Holm correction (p = 0.106); the other five did. Within neural models, VAE n = 3 beat CAE and CVAE under BH, while CAE n = 8 beat both variational models. Condition-only Ridge MSE was 1.233795.',[2,1,1])
    a.p('Generation showed a different pattern. VAE and CVAE produced smaller feature discrepancies than spline + PCA at both capacities (Table 2). At eight dimensions, VAE had the lowest numerical mean for all three reported feature metrics. Compared with CVAE, however, its energy advantage was significant only under BH (q = 0.0257), not Holm; KS (q = 0.137) and MMD (q = 0.0518) did not cross the BH threshold. These results support VAE as a practical default without establishing a uniquely superior variational architecture.')
    table(a,[['Model','n','Mean KS','Energy','MMD squared']]+result_rows(['mean_ks','energy_distance','mmd_rbf']),'<b>Table 2.</b> Generation fidelity on eleven kinematic features; all entries are participant means and lower is better. VAE and CVAE each beat spline + PCA on all three metrics at n = 3 and n = 8 under both BH and Holm correction over 140 tests. CAE n = 8 did not differ significantly from spline on these metrics. Feature energy/MMD use standardized eleven-feature vectors and cannot be numerically compared with the trajectory distances in Table 3. Full selected comparisons appear in Appendix A7.',[1.7,.4,1,1,1])
    a.p('Among compact models at n = 3, CVAE had the lowest mean KS (0.2778), whereas VAE had the lowest joint MMD (0.1303). This metric dependence reinforces the need to state the endpoint and capacity when naming a winner. The condition-only Ridge reference had mean KS 0.2773, energy 0.8187 and MMD 0.1259. It was included in the original correction family and remains available in Appendix A7. Its comparatively plausible feature summaries, despite poor reconstruction, also motivate comparing whole paths.')
    a.p('The feature measures address complementary properties. Mean KS evaluates each marginal distribution separately, whereas energy and MMD assess the joint eleven-dimensional distribution. Correlated features can place extra emphasis on related properties in the joint distances. We retained the specified feature set for this analysis; reducing it after inspecting results would define a new analysis rather than repair the existing p-values. No per-feature KS p-values were combined into the reported mean KS.')
    a.page()
    a.h('6 Full-trajectory generation')
    a.p('A generator could match selected movement features while missing details of the path. We therefore evaluated the complete ordered 100-point trajectories from the same frozen generations. At eight dimensions, VAE and CVAE both improved on spline + PCA in energy and MMD (Table 3). Their raw-trajectory energy means were almost identical. This extends the feature result to the distribution of phase-normalized paths, while leaving physical-time fidelity a separate question.')
    table(a,[['Model','n','Trajectory energy','Trajectory MMD squared']]+result_rows(['energy','mmd2'],'raw_rms'),'<b>Table 3.</b> Full-trajectory distribution discrepancies in the primary RMS geometry. At n = 8, VAE and CVAE each beat spline + PCA on both metrics under BH and Holm correction across all 80 tests. VAE versus CVAE was not significant (BH q = 0.672 for energy and 1.000 for MMD). At n = 3, each variational model beat spline on MMD (BH q = 0.00184, Holm p = 0.0363), but not energy (VAE q = 0.0647; CVAE q = 0.205). Lower is better.',[1.6,.35,1.4,1.65])
    a.figure(ASSETS/'trajectory_energy.png','<b>Figure 2.</b> Cohort-average full-trajectory energy for all four compact models. The n = 8 VAE/CVAE advantages over spline survive both corrections and the axis-balanced sensitivity analysis (Appendix A8). At n = 3 their energy advantages are not significant, and their MMD advantages do not survive the axis-balanced BH analysis. Bars summarize 28 participants after seed averaging; they do not show uncertainty or a formal n = 3 versus n = 8 test.',60*mm)
    a.p('An empirical reference that resampled a participant\'s stored context paths achieved mean energy 0.0328 and MMD 0.0054, below the compact models\' means. This reference retains complete trajectories instead of an n-value center, so it addresses a different memory constraint, but it demonstrates that the compact generator does not yet recover all available session information. These reference differences are descriptive, without pairwise significance tests.')
    a.p('Repeating the context mean path gave a smaller mean-path RMSE (0.1146) than VAE n = 8 (0.1604), yet far worse energy (0.4621 versus 0.0548) and essentially zero generated variation. Thus distance to a mean path, or average error to all recorded paths, cannot alone establish successful stochastic generation. Distribution comparisons reward both agreement and an appropriate spread, rather than allowing an average-only output to appear sufficient.')
    a.page()
    a.h('7 Personal information and the dashboard')
    a.p('Good generation does not by itself show that the compact center carries information about its owner. We therefore held the decoder, task draws, covariance and random variation fixed, and replaced the center with either the training-population center or centers from other test participants. Using the participant\'s own context improved feature distributions for CVAE at both capacities and for VAE at eight dimensions (Figure 3). The VAE comparisons covered six tests and the CVAE comparisons twelve; every comparison favored the own-context center after BH correction. This supports useful personal information within a session, rather than stability across separate sessions.')
    a.figure(ASSETS/'fingerprint_controls.png','<b>Figure 3.</b> Mean KS under replacement of the latent center; lower is better. VAE n = 8 means were 0.2151 (own), 0.2841 (population) and 0.3144 (other participants). All six VAE comparisons across KS, energy and MMD had BH q &lt; 1.6 x 10<super>-6</super>; all twelve CVAE comparisons had q &lt; 0.000382. Control bars use saved study means. These feature controls do not establish that each latent coordinate has an interpretable meaning, and were not repeated for the new full-path endpoints.',55*mm)
    a.figure(ASSETS/'generated_paths.png','<b>Figure 4.</b> Recorded query and generated trajectories for subject01 at n = 8 (fold 2; neural seed 42). The first 30 stored paths illustrate spread; solid curves show means of all stored paths in each panel. Subject01 was chosen by identifier, without ranking model scores. Each panel uses equal x/y scale and shared limits. These examples illustrate outputs; inference uses all 28 participants. Individual generated paths have no one-to-one pairing with recorded trials.',65*mm)
    a.p('The Streamlit dashboard integrates generation, visual exploration and benchmark comparisons. It supports CVAE and spline + PCA at n = 2, 3, 4 and 8, and CAE and VAE at n = 3 and 8. VAE n = 8 is the default because it combines the lowest mean feature discrepancies with the strong full-path results. CVAE remains a supported alternative, particularly when inspecting explicit task conditions. Spline + PCA provides the statistical comparison and strongest mean reconstruction. The VAE ignores condition controls; spline conditions affect timing only.')
    a.p('Live references use fold 0 and neural seed 42, while study score cards summarize the evaluated rotations and available seeds. The interface labels these scopes. The dashboard demonstrates the fitted models and does not supply independent validation of model selection. Appendix A9 gives reproducible launch instructions and a short demonstration sequence. No n = 16 model was evaluated in the matched study.')
    a.page()
    a.h('8 Discussion')
    a.p('The results separate faithful compression of an observed trajectory from useful generation of movement variability. Spline + PCA retained trajectory shape most accurately, while variational models at eight dimensions produced closer feature and whole-path distributions. This difference is consistent with the distinct tasks, but the experiments do not isolate a single causal explanation for the ranking. Our practical selection is VAE n = 8, with CVAE retained as a statistically comparable alternative for full-path generation. The original three-coordinate objective was only partly achieved: personal context was useful, but distribution fidelity and robustness were stronger at eight dimensions.')
    a.p('The evidence has several boundaries. Context and query trials came from the same recording session, and shared a task-condition mixture. Cross-session stability, unseen task extrapolation and a psychological interpretation of latent coordinates remain open. The noise covariance was shared across participants, and context averaging discarded information retained by empirical resampling. Direct context summaries also outperformed latent linear probes on the fourteen tested behavioral-summary targets (Appendix A10). We therefore regard the fingerprint as a useful representation for this decoder, rather than a sufficient representation of every measured individual difference.')
    a.p('Timing reconstruction is especially relevant to derivative-based features. Phase normalization does not encode seconds directly, and generated features depend on predicted durations and an onset-based crop. The event audit identified an arrival-proxy offset, zero-initiation trials and sensitivity to onset assumptions. Extreme timing predictions were retained in the evaluation, including an approximately 11.5-second initiation prediction in one CVAE n = 8 run. The full-path follow-up avoids the generated movement crop, but assesses paths in normalized phase and cannot validate physical timing by itself.')
    a.p('The comparisons are exploratory. Participants were held out from model fitting, but rotations shared training participants, three neural seeds do not exhaust training variability, and the dashboard default was chosen using the evaluated cohort. The n = 3 results depended on the distance metric and axis scaling. Nonsignificant VAE-CVAE differences do not show that task conditions are irrelevant: conditioning routes were active, and these architectures and settings are only one test of their value. A new cohort or prospectively held-out model-selection evaluation would strengthen the ranking. Feature reduction should be justified using the task and training data, with results reassessed under a specified protocol rather than selected to improve significance.')
    a.h('9 Conclusion')
    a.p('A compact context-derived representation can improve generation of a participant\'s interception-movement distribution, but reconstruction and generation favor different models. VAE and CVAE at eight dimensions provide the strongest compact full-path generation evidence relative to spline + PCA. The dashboard makes these models accessible, while the empirical resampling reference and unsuccessful summary probes identify information still lost through compression. Further work can examine richer participant distributions, condition-specific fidelity and generalization to new sessions.')
    a.h('References')
    refs=[
      '[1] Kingma, D. P., and Welling, M. (2014). Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes. ICLR. arXiv:1312.6114.',
      '[2] Sohn, K., Lee, H., and Yan, X. (2015). Learning Structured Output Representation using Deep Conditional Generative Models. NeurIPS 28.',
      '[3] Szekely, G. J., and Rizzo, M. L. (2013). Energy statistics: A class of statistics based on distances. Journal of Statistical Planning and Inference 143, 1249-1272.',
      '[4] Gretton, A., et al. (2012). A Kernel Two-Sample Test. Journal of Machine Learning Research 13, 723-773.',
      '[5] Benjamini, Y., and Hochberg, Y. (1995). Controlling the false discovery rate: A practical and powerful approach to multiple testing. JRSS B 57, 289-300.'
    ]
    for r in refs:a.p(r,'caption')

def write_appendix(a):
    a.h('A1 Data, time axes and experimental conditions')
    a.p('This appendix supports the eight-page course report. The frozen 31-page laboratory report retains the broader investigations and outstanding laboratory questions. Numerical results here come from the same saved studies, plus the September 13 trajectory-distribution follow-up. No new neural training was needed for that follow-up.')
    table(a,[['Stage','Input','Output and purpose'],['Loading','CSV frame counters, 3-D positions; paired MATLAB target trace','Trial objects with task metadata and event alignment'],['Regularization','Irregular/repeated frame entries','240 Hz grid after duplicate averaging and linear gap filling'],['Filtering','Regular 3-D positions','Fourth-order 10 Hz forward-backward Butterworth positions'],['Window','Target-motion onset and final tracker sample','Go-to-end path, including waiting after target motion'],['Resampling','Variable-length filtered path','100 phase samples, then translation to a zero start'],['Planar input','100 x 3 array','x-y array of 100 x 2; 200 time-interleaved values for neural models']],'<b>Table A1.</b> Preprocessing stages. The onset-to-end crop used for features is distinct from the go-to-end window supplied to the trajectory models.',[1,1.6,2.4])
    a.p('The condition vector contains three one-hot starting-category entries, a side indicator and measured target speed divided by the 1920-pixel screen width. The nominal category settings were 120, 140 and 160, associated with speed ranges 255-300, 298-350 and 340-400 mm/s in the laboratory stimulus description. The executed speed supplied to the encoder is derived from the target trace, not a one-hot speed class. Starting category and speed are coupled by design.')
    a.p('The appearance-to-motion foreperiod ranged from 0.20 to 0.50 s (median 0.35 s). The current modeled window begins at target motion, so it retains early movement already present at that point but excludes earlier appearance-to-motion kinematics. A three-frame threshold crossing in 3-D speed identifies finger onset. When no crossing is found, code falls back to target motion; a zero label can also occur when the threshold is crossed at the start. These cases must not be equated with noise or a behavioral strategy.')
    for q in ['Confirm tracker spatial units and that x-y is the task plane.','Confirm the arrival proxy, its offset and the target-motion alignment.','Confirm onset threshold, persistence, fallback and whether the earlier appearance period should be modeled.','Confirm incomplete recording availability for subject02 and subject41, the late-arrival exclusion and retained fixation warnings.','Confirm units and reference origin for nominal starting settings 120/140/160.']:
        a.p('<font color="#b22222">[Jason: '+q+']</font>')
    a.p('Source: src/data_loading.py, src/preprocessing.py, src/trajectory_view.py; production/cohort_trial_counts.csv and data_figure_provenance.json. The paper uses tracker units until the physical spatial unit is confirmed. These open questions do not change the numerical comparison of methods run on the same current representation.')
    a.page();a.h('A2 Spline + PCA: the complete matrix pipeline')
    a.p('For one trial, x and y each have 100 samples. A fixed cubic B-spline basis with five interior knots gives B of shape 100 x 9. Least squares solves B c approximately equal to x separately for each axis. The 18 coefficients are ordered as all nine x values followed by all nine y values. This is a different ordering from the time-interleaved neural input.')
    eq(a,'spline');eq(a,'pca')
    table(a,[['Object','Shape','Meaning'],['B','100 x 9','One common spline design matrix'],['c','18','Coefficients for one trial: nine x, nine y'],['C training','N training x 18','One row per training trial'],['V','18 x n','PCA directions learned from training rows'],['Z training','N training x n','Compressed training codes'],['Decoded coefficients','18','Inverse scale/center applied after PCA inverse'],['Decoded path','100 x 2','B times the two reconstructed coefficient blocks']],'<b>Table A2.</b> n is 3 or 8 in the matched report. The number of retained PCA components is not the number of knots.',[1.3,1.2,2.5])
    a.p('Raw-coefficient PCA centers the coefficients but does not standardize each coefficient. The standardized variant additionally divides each coefficient by its training standard deviation. The implementation evaluates both variants on validation trajectory reconstruction MSE. PCA maximizes retained variance in its coefficient representation; it does not mathematically guarantee minimum trajectory MSE across every competing model. The validation choice matters because coefficient distance and sampled-path distance differ.')
    a.p('At reconstruction time the query trajectory is available, so its spline coefficients may be fitted before projection into the already learned PCA axes. No PCA axes or scalers are fitted to query data. At generation time only context coefficients produce the center; the query path is unavailable. Applying the inverse PCA transform produces coefficients directly, without fitting a spline to a query trajectory.')
    a.h('Timing regression',True)
    a.p('The timing design has n + 5 inputs per trial: its PCA scores and task conditions. Training-standardized inputs feed a Ridge regression with intercept, predicting two training-standardized log durations. Alpha is selected from 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10 and 100 by validation MSE in standardized log-duration space. The fitted model remains based on the 17 training participants. Timing targets are not appended to the trajectory PCA coefficient matrix in the final evaluated configuration.')
    eq(a,'ridge')
    a.p('Source: src/confirmatory_spline.py and the final strategy evaluation runner. In the displayed objective U is the standardized input matrix, Y the two-column target matrix, W the regression weights, and b the unpenalized intercept. Generation can therefore change spline durations with task conditions even though the spatial spline decoder ignores them.')
    a.page();a.h('A3 Neural objective and inference stages')
    a.p('All coordinate means and scales are learned on training trials. Each neural encoder receives 200 standardized coordinates and five condition slots. CVAE and CAE use the actual slots; VAE sets them to zero at both encoder and decoder. Two hidden layers of 256 ReLU units produce a deterministic code (CAE), or separate mean and log-variance heads (VAE/CVAE). Variational training draws standard-normal epsilon and reparameterizes the latent sample. The decoder has two 256-unit ReLU layers and coordinate and timing heads.')
    eq(a,'sample');eq(a,'loss');eq(a,'kl')
    a.p('The reconstruction term sums 200 squared standardized coordinate errors. The timing term sums two squared standardized log-duration errors and multiplies by 20. KL sums over n latent dimensions. The batch then averages the per-trial objective. For CAE the KL term is absent. Beta starts at zero and reaches one at epoch 51. Validation uses the full beta = 1 objective, avoiding a different target simply because the training warm-up is incomplete.')
    table(a,[['Stage','Encoder sees','Latent value','Outputs and use'],['Training','Observed training shape; conditions where enabled','Posterior draw, or deterministic CAE code','Coordinate and timing losses plus KL where applicable'],['Reconstruction','Observed test shape; conditions where enabled','Posterior mean, or deterministic code','Path compared with this observed path'],['Context encoding','Context shapes only; their conditions where enabled','Mean code for every context trial','Average codes into participant center'],['Generation','No query path enters encoder','Draw around context center with training covariance','New path and predicted durations']],'<b>Table A3.</b> The word VAE does not imply that all evaluation stages sample from the standard-normal training prior.',[1,1.6,1.4,1.6])
    a.p('Adam uses learning rate 0.001, batch size 64 and gradient clipping at norm 5. Weighted sampling gives each training participant equal expected contribution. Validation controls learning-rate reduction, checkpoint selection and early stopping with patience 25, for at most 150 epochs. The three random seeds quantify some training variation; they are averaged within a participant before model tests and are not counted as independent participants.')
    a.p('Durations are inverse-standardized and converted by exp(log target) - 0.001, with negative outputs clipped to zero. The generation feature pipeline additionally floors movement duration at 1 ms and limits the initiation fraction used for cropping to 0.98. No upper clipping removed extreme positive predictions from the main evaluation. The coordinate encoder never receives the two observed timing labels.')
    a.p('Source: src/vae_model.py, src/train.py, src/confirmatory_evaluation.py and scripts/run_review_controls.py. Condition masking is an implemented ablation; it does not establish that conditions have no relation to human movement.')
    a.page();a.h('A4 Context enrollment and stochastic generation')
    a.p('A test participant belongs to one held-out rotation. A fixed stratified split allocates approximately half of the participant\'s trials to context and half to query. Each model uses that same assignment. Averaging context posterior means produces theta; spline and CAE use their deterministic codes. A participant can therefore be unseen during population training while still supplying recordings for enrollment.')
    eq(a,'fingerprint');eq(a,'covariance')
    a.p('The first covariance term pools deviations of each training code from that training participant\'s average code. For variational models the diagonal of mean posterior variances is added; spline and CAE omit it. All models add 10<super>-6</super>I. The participant\'s context code center changes at enrollment, while this training covariance and the decoder remain fixed. The training sample covariance is not a separate variance estimated from each test participant\'s few context trials.')
    a.p('Each participant receives 120 generated trials. The generation procedure uses task conditions sampled from the query-condition mixture, allowing a matched-condition evaluation. It withholds query positions and durations. VAE ignores the sampled task conditions, spline uses them for timing, and CVAE/CAE use them in decoding. This is not a test of extrapolation to unseen conditions. Three neural training seeds are averaged; spline uses its single frozen fitting and sampling realization.')
    a.p('The trajectory follow-up reproduced the previous feature scores from the generated arrays to machine precision before evaluating new endpoints. It reused 72 frozen neural checkpoints for n = 3/8 and recreated the classical fits. Condition Ridge required the historical two-thread numerical setting to reproduce the near-singular fit. No neural training changed, and the laboratory report remained frozen. Saved arrays under production/assets/trajectory_distribution_2026_09_13 allow the spatial analysis to be repeated without retraining when the local cache is available.')
    a.h('Personal-center replacement control',True)
    a.p('The own-context center is compared with the training-population center and the average performance of six other test-participant centers from the same rotation. Decoder, covariance, random latent noise and task draws remain fixed. This tests whether changing the center to the correct participant improves this generator. It does not test long-term stability or identify physical meanings for the individual latent axes. The control was evaluated on feature distances for CVAE n = 3/8 and VAE n = 8, not for every model/capacity and not for the new full-trajectory endpoints.')
    a.p('Sources: src/context_query.py, scripts/run_review_controls.py and production/trajectory_distribution_2026_09_13/protocol.json. Generation inherits the earlier character-sum participant seed convention, which can give different identifiers the same random-number stream. The saved protocol discloses this reproducibility limitation. It does not expose query trajectories to model fitting.')
    a.page();a.h('A5 The eleven feature distributions')
    table(a,[['Feature','Definition / role'],['Initiation time','Seconds from target motion to detected finger onset'],['Movement time','Seconds from detected finger onset to final sample'],['Peak speed','Largest planar speed after feature-stage processing'],['Relative time to peak speed','Peak-speed time expressed as fraction of movement duration'],['Path length','Sum of planar segment lengths'],['Straight-line distance','Distance between movement-segment start and end'],['Curvature index','Path length divided by straight-line distance'],['Maximum lateral deviation','Largest deviation from the start-to-end straight line'],['Speed-peak count','Heuristic peaks with prominence 10% of maximum speed and about 50 ms separation; minimum reported count one'],['Endpoint x','Final x coordinate'],['Endpoint y','Final y coordinate']],'<b>Table A4.</b> The count is a speed-peak heuristic, not the number of fitted minimum-jerk components.',[1.7,3.3])
    a.p('Recorded features use the observed onset-to-end segment and its measured duration. Generated features crop the go-to-end trajectory using predicted initiation/(initiation + movement time), capped at 0.98. Both feature representations contain 100 movement-phase points. Their physical sampling rate is 99 divided by movement duration. The feature-stage low-pass cutoff is min(10 Hz, 0.45 times that sampling rate), and numerical gradients provide velocities. Matching this downstream processing was essential for fair recorded/generated comparisons.')
    eq(a,'ks')
    a.p('An empirical cumulative distribution F(x) is the fraction of observations at or below x. For one feature and participant, KS is the largest vertical separation between the recorded and generated cumulative curves. We report its statistic, which ranges from zero to one. We then average eleven statistics within a participant. This is not an average of eleven p-values, and the BH family does not contain eleven separate KS tests per participant.')
    a.p('Energy and MMD instead receive the entire eleven-value feature vector per trial. Training means and standard deviations put these quantities on comparable numerical scales, with the peak-count scale floored at one. Correlation still matters: redundant coordinates can emphasize the same underlying property in a Euclidean distance. Removing correlated features is a legitimate future sensitivity analysis if the selection rule is specified from training data, but it changes the multivariate endpoint. It should not be chosen merely to obtain smaller corrected p-values.')
    a.p('Sources: src/features.py, src/confirmatory_evaluation.py and corrected feature-processing paths in scripts/run_review_controls.py. Timing errors and spatial errors can both change the generated features, whereas the full go-to-end path distances in the new analysis do not use predicted durations to crop trajectories.')
    a.page();a.h('A6 Distances and inferential statistics')
    eq(a,'energy');eq(a,'mmd')
    a.p('For recorded vectors X and generated vectors Y, energy uses twice the mean cross Euclidean distance minus the two mean within-sample distances. All ordered within pairs, including diagonal zeros, enter this empirical V-statistic. There is no extra square root of the resulting statistic. Subtracting within-sample distances matters: a collapsed mean path can have small distance to many observations yet fail to reproduce their variation.')
    a.p('MMD uses k(x,y) = exp(-gamma times squared Euclidean distance). Each within-sample term excludes the diagonal and divides by the number of ordered distinct pairs, whereas the cross term includes every pair. This unbiased squared estimator can be slightly negative at finite sample sizes; it is not a negative physical distance. Gamma is the inverse median positive squared distance among a deterministic sample of at most 512 training trials. Feature and path analyses use their own training representations and fixed bandwidths, shared by all models within a rotation.')
    a.p('Raw path vectors contain the flattened ordered 100 x 2 coordinates divided by sqrt(200). Each Euclidean pair distance therefore equals RMS coordinate difference in tracker units. The axis-balanced sensitivity additionally divides x and y by their respective training RMS deviations around the training mean path. It does not standardize every time point separately or align paths by dynamic time warping. Feature geometry instead uses its eleven training-standardized features. Absolute values across these representations should not be directly compared.')
    a.h('The unit of inference',True)
    a.p('For a model pair we have 28 paired participant scores after averaging seeds within participant. The Pratt Wilcoxon procedure ranks absolute paired differences, retaining zeros in ranking but excluding their signed contribution. A two-sided test evaluates a signed-rank null; its usual location interpretation relies on symmetry of the difference distribution. This is not a normality test or a test on thousands of supposedly independent trials. Rounding differences to 12 decimals prevents numerical round-off alone from breaking ties.')
    a.p('BH sorts p-values and controls the false-discovery rate under its applicable dependence assumptions. Holm provides a more conservative familywise-error sensitivity. Neither correction makes overlapping trained models or cohort-based model selection independent. We report exploratory evidence and both corrections rather than interpreting a threshold as proof. Nonsignificance is not equivalence. The seven original endpoints were trajectory MSE, initiation MAE, movement MAE, mean KS, speed-peak-count KS, feature energy and feature MMD squared.')
    table(a,[['Family','Number','Definition'],['Original model comparisons','140','10 model pairs x 2 dimensions x 7 endpoints'],['New path comparisons','80','10 pairs x 2 dimensions x 2 metrics x 2 geometries'],['CVAE center controls','12','2 dimensions x 2 replacement controls x 3 feature metrics'],['VAE center controls','6','n=8 x 2 replacement controls x 3 feature metrics']],'<b>Table A5.</b> Separate analysis families. The new 80-test correction includes both geometries together. No new n=3-versus-n=8 or empirical-reference significance test is claimed.',[1.5,.5,3])
    a.page();a.h('A7 Selected model comparisons and exact corrected values')
    a.p('Every row compares VAE with the named model at the same dimension. Direction must be read with the reported means in the main paper. q denotes BH over the full original family of 140 tests; Holm p uses that same family. Display rounding does not determine significance.')
    names={'trajectory_mse':'Reconstruction MSE','mean_ks':'Mean feature KS','energy_distance':'Feature energy','mmd_rbf':'Feature MMD squared'}
    rows=[['n','Comparator','Endpoint','BH q','Holm p']]
    for n,f,m,q,h in selected_tests(): rows.append([n,f,names[m],f'{q:.3g}',f'{h:.3g}'])
    table(a,rows,'<b>Table A6.</b> All 24 VAE comparisons against the other three compact models on the four main original endpoints.',[.35,1.05,1.65,.85,.85])
    a.p('The condition-only Ridge reference had MSE 1.233795, mean feature KS 0.277264, feature energy 0.818722 and feature MMD squared 0.125854. Its full-path raw energy was 0.252014 and MMD squared 0.109010. It has no latent dimension and is reused in both dimension-specific comparison families. Complete all-pairs values remain in audit_2026_09_08/all_pairs_140.csv and trajectory_distribution_2026_09_13/paired_comparisons.csv.')
    a.page();a.h('A8 Full-path robustness and empirical references')
    table(a,[['Model','n','Axis-balanced energy','Axis-balanced MMD squared']]+result_rows(['energy','mmd2'],'axis_balanced'),'<b>Table A7.</b> Axis-balanced sensitivity using training-only scales. Lower is better.',[1.4,.35,1.65,1.6])
    rows=[['n','Pair','Metric','Geometry','BH q','Holm p']]
    for n in (3,8):
        for f in ('cvae','unconditional_vae'):
            for g in ('raw_rms','axis_balanced'):
                for m in ('energy','mmd2'):
                    r=comparison('spline_pca',f,n,m,g)
                    rows.append([str(n),LABELS[f]+' / spline',m,'Raw' if g=='raw_rms' else 'Balanced',f'{r.q_bh_80:.3g}',f'{r.p_holm_80:.3g}'])
    table(a,rows,'<b>Table A8.</b> Variational models versus spline, corrected over all 80 path tests. At n = 8 every listed advantage survives Holm. The n = 3 conclusion changes with metric/scaling.',[.3,1.3,.7,.8,.75,.75])
    a.p('Stored-context resampling selected 120 paths with replacement for each participant and averaged three stable SHA-derived random seeds. In raw geometry it achieved energy 0.032847 and MMD squared 0.005407. Repeating the context mean gave energy 0.462058 and MMD squared 0.245241. Neither reference compresses a participant into n numbers, and neither comparison received a significance test. The mean-only reference is a collapse diagnostic, not a candidate stochastic model.')
    a.p('The independent verifier checked 184 decoded-array cases and all 80 paired tests. Existing feature scores were reproduced to a largest absolute difference of 4.44 x 10<super>-16</super>. These computational checks establish consistency with the saved analysis, not independent scientific replication. Protocol, complete scores and verification records accompany the results folder.')
    a.page();a.h('A9 Dashboard and reproducibility')
    a.p('The dashboard defaults to VAE n = 8, the lowest mean feature-discrepancy configuration in the matched comparison. CVAE and spline offer n = 2, 3, 4, 8, while CAE and VAE offer n = 3, 8. These are the evaluated model choices; no n = 16 result is exposed. Models have different conditioning behavior, so moving a condition control is not a validated causal intervention on human behavior.')
    a.p('The Generate section shows live examples from fold 0, seed 42. Validation distributions use the available exported seed-42 examples across four rotations, while score cards summarize the study seeds. A demonstration should distinguish these illustrative trajectories from cohort-level significance. The report\'s subject01 figure uses fold 2 and therefore need not match the live fold-0 examples.')
    a.h('PowerShell launch',True)
    a.p('From the repository folder, run:<br/><font face="Courier" size="8">python -m streamlit run src/confirmatory_dashboard.py --server.port 8510 --server.address 127.0.0.1 --browser.gatherUsageStats false</font>')
    a.p('Open http://127.0.0.1:8510 in a browser. Keep the terminal open during the demonstration and press Ctrl+C there to stop the server. The standalone archive in output/release includes its own launch instructions when a full checkout and local training caches are unavailable.')
    a.h('Suggested short demonstration',True)
    a.p('Show VAE n = 8 and explain that the displayed paths are samples around a context-derived center. Change to n = 3 to illustrate the compact setting, without claiming this single example proves the cohort result. Show CVAE as the conditional alternative and spline + PCA as the statistical comparator. Finish on the benchmark view to return from individual trajectories to the study-level evidence.')
    table(a,[['Evidence or implementation','Location relative to repository'],['Main means and original tests','production/audit_2026_09_08/all_model_means.csv; all_pairs_140.csv'],['New trajectory analysis','production/trajectory_distribution_2026_09_13/'],['New analysis implementation','scripts/analyze_trajectory_distributions.py; src/trajectory_distribution.py'],['Generation and original evaluation','src/context_query.py; src/confirmatory_evaluation.py; scripts/run_review_controls.py'],['Model definitions','src/vae_model.py; src/baseline_spline.py; src/confirmatory_spline.py'],['Dashboard','src/confirmatory_dashboard.py; src/dashboard_models.py'],['Frozen extended report','production/Interception_Movements_Methods_Reconstruction_Review.pdf']],'<b>Table A9.</b> Navigation for checking methods against implementation and stored results.',[1.7,3.3])
    a.page();a.h('A10 Secondary investigations and remaining interpretation')
    a.h('Timing and behavioral-summary probes',True)
    a.p('Timing heads typically had mean absolute errors of tens of milliseconds, while occasional large errors affected variance-explained measures. For VAE n = 8, initiation MAE was 35.98 ms and movement MAE 42.21 ms; the corresponding spline values were 55.58 and 50.21 ms. These timing reconstructions receive the observed shape and therefore are not prospective timing forecasts before a movement begins. Common-head comparisons in the laboratory report assess how changing the timing head changes representation comparisons.')
    a.p('Linear probes attempted to predict fourteen participant summaries: means and standard deviations of seven behavioral features. Directly computing those summaries on context trials yielded lower MAE on every target than each of the eight model/dimension probe configurations. In the pooled summaries, nine of fourteen VAE n = 8 targets and ten of fourteen CVAE n = 3 targets had negative R-squared. A useful center for a nonlinear generator therefore does not establish reliable linear access to every individual behavioral statistic. See production/all_models_direct_context_summary.csv and the frozen report, pages 18-22.')
    a.h('Conditioning and movement decomposition',True)
    a.p('Code checks verified that CVAE and CAE receive conditions at the intended inputs. Shuffling decoder conditions worsened mean reconstruction, so the routes were active. Condition-stratified feature tests did not establish corrected fidelity differences. These results neither prove a coding failure nor prove that experimental conditions are irrelevant. A larger causal conclusion would require a different evaluation design.')
    a.p('Minimum-jerk analyses fitted overlapping smooth velocity pulses under a matched downstream procedure. Their component-count total-variation discrepancies were approximately 0.214 (CVAE n = 3), 0.232 (CVAE n = 8) and 0.236 (VAE n = 8). None of the nine matched pairwise comparisons was significant after correction. Counts depended on fit assumptions and remained distinct from the simpler speed-peak feature used in the main report. The broader decomposition, event audit, clustering and enrollment experiments remain in the frozen report, pages 18-28, for laboratory discussion.')
    a.h('Scope for the next study',True)
    a.p('Promising follow-ups include a prespecified feature-redundancy analysis, richer participant-specific latent distributions, prospective evaluation after model selection, and testing across sessions or new task conditions. No current learning-curve experiment establishes that sample size caused the failures. Retaining a failed probe or a strong empirical reference helps identify what the compact model loses, rather than weakening the scientific value of reporting the actual results.')
    a.p('The course report selects the experiments that establish the modeling task, compare deep and statistical methods, evaluate generation and justify the dashboard. The laboratory report preserves the extended evidence while Jason reviews the experimental assumptions. Neither document substitutes for a new validation cohort.')
